{
 "type": "FeatureCollection",
 "metadata": {
  "note": "The Rock Island's Chicago - Peoria line, and the Peoria Rocket that ran it. Peoria was one of the map's measured coverage holes - the nearest drawn passenger line was 26 km away in every era - even though this route carried trains from 1855 to the last day of 1978. The line is two railroads end to end: the Rock Island's own Chicago - Joliet - Bureau main line, and the Peoria and Bureau Valley Railroad, 46.9 miles from Bureau to Peoria, opened February 1 1855 and leased to the Chicago and Rock Island in perpetuity from the start - so it is drawn as one Rock Island feature rather than split by owner.",
  "sources": [
   "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Peoria_Rocket",
   "https://www.american-rails.com/peoria.html",
   "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:WikiProject_Trains/ICC_valuations/Peoria_and_Bureau_Valley_Railroad",
   "data/metra.geojson (existing layer, Chicago - Joliet geometry)"
  ],
  "geometry_sources": [
   "Two sources joined at Joliet with a 0.7 km gap: Chicago - Joliet reuses the Metra Rock Island District geometry already in data/metra.geojson (the same Rock Island railroad, still in commuter service), and Joliet - Morris - Ottawa - LaSalle - Bureau - Chillicothe - Peoria is traced for this layer from OpenStreetMap relations 2221757 (CSX New Rock Subdivision), 17497925 (Illinois Railway Ottawa Branch), 2293486 / 8812539 (Iowa Interstate) and 17497736 (Iowa Interstate Peoria Subdivision), stitched into one graph and routed with Dijkstra, then Douglas-Peucker simplified at 15 m."
  ],
  "not_yet_sourced": [
   "Service levels before 1937 are unknown: headway_min for the 1855-1937 segment is a flat estimate and no Official Guide was consulted.",
   "That the Rock Island stayed out of Amtrak in 1971 and therefore had to keep running its own passenger trains is well known but is not stated in the sources consulted here; the segment note gives only what they support - that the Peoria Rocket ran until December 31 1978, seven years after Amtrak began.",
   "The Chicago and North Western, Illinois Central, Peoria and Pekin Union and Toledo, Peoria and Western also served Peoria and are not drawn."
  ],
  "passenger_only_rule": "Drawn to December 31 1978, the Peoria Rocket's last run. Freight still moves over the line (Iowa Interstate and CSX) and Metra still runs Chicago - Joliet; neither is drawn here."
 },
 "features": [
  {
   "type": "Feature",
   "properties": {
    "company": "Chicago, Rock Island & Pacific Railroad",
    "line": "Chicago, Rock Island & Pacific: Chicago – Peoria",
    "id": "crip-chicago-peoria",
    "color": "#D62839",
    "mode": "intercity_rail",
    "avg_speed_kmh": 97,
    "speed_source": "The Peoria Rocket covered the 161-mile route in a little over two and a half hours, averaging 60.5 mph = 97 km/h, per american-rails.com. That is the streamliner figure; 1855-1937 running was far slower and this feature carries the fast number throughout.",
    "segments": [
     {
      "start": 1855,
      "end": 1937,
      "headway_min": 720,
      "note": "The Peoria and Bureau Valley Railroad - incorporated February 12 1853, built under a contract of May 27 1853, opened for operation February 1 1855 and handed straight to the Chicago and Rock Island Rail Road, which leased it in perpetuity under an agreement of April 14 1854 - runs 46.9 miles from Bureau to Peoria. With the Rock Island's own main line from Chicago through Joliet, LaSalle and Bureau it made a Chicago - Peoria route from 1855 on. headway_min is an unsourced estimate.",
      "sources": [
       "Segment-level: the 1853 incorporation, the May 27 1853 construction contract, the February 1 1855 opening, the perpetual lease of April 14 1854 and the 46.946-mile length are from the ICC valuation summary of the Peoria and Bureau Valley Railroad. headway_min and avg_speed_kmh are estimates.",
       "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:WikiProject_Trains/ICC_valuations/Peoria_and_Bureau_Valley_Railroad"
      ]
     },
     {
      "start": 1937,
      "end": 1970,
      "headway_min": 720,
      "note": "The Peoria Rocket entered service on September 19 1937, one of the Rock Island's first streamliners: a four-car set - dinette 400 Joliet, coach 306 Ottawa, coach 300 La Salle and parlor-buffet-observation 450 Peoria - making two round trips a day over the 161 miles in a little over two and a half hours, 60.5 mph start to stop. headway_min 720 reflects those two round trips each way.",
      "sources": [
       "Segment-level: the September 19 1937 inaugural, the 161-mile route, the two daily round trips, the 60.5 mph average and the named cars in the original consist are from american-rails.com's Peoria Rocket page.",
       "https://www.american-rails.com/peoria.html",
       "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Peoria_Rocket"
      ]
     },
     {
      "start": 1970,
      "end": 1978,
      "headway_min": 1440,
      "note": "Passenger service here outlived Amtrak's 1971 start: the Peoria Rocket kept running under the Rock Island itself until December 31 1978, one of the last privately operated intercity passenger trains in the United States. A state-supported successor, the Prairie Marksman, ran the corridor briefly in 1980-81 and is not drawn here.",
      "sources": [
       "Segment-level: the December 31 1978 discontinuance and the 1980-81 Prairie Marksman successor are from the Wikipedia Peoria Rocket article. headway_min is an estimate for the final years.",
       "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Peoria_Rocket"
      ]
     }
    ],
    "geometry_source": "Two sources joined at Joliet with a 0.7 km gap: Chicago - Joliet reuses the Metra Rock Island District geometry already in data/metra.geojson (the same Rock Island railroad, still in commuter service), and Joliet - Morris - Ottawa - LaSalle - Bureau - Chillicothe - Peoria is traced for this layer from OpenStreetMap relations 2221757 (CSX New Rock Subdivision), 17497925 (Illinois Railway Ottawa Branch), 2293486 / 8812539 (Iowa Interstate) and 17497736 (Iowa Interstate Peoria Subdivision), stitched into one graph and routed with Dijkstra, then Douglas-Peucker simplified at 15 m.",
    "geometry_quality": "158.8 miles end to end against the documented 161-mile Peoria Rocket route, of which 118.2 miles Joliet - Peoria is this layer's own OSM trace (passing within 1.1 km of Morris, Ottawa, LaSalle, Bureau and Chillicothe) and the rest is the existing Metra Rock Island District geometry from Chicago to Joliet. The two are joined by a 0.7 km straight line at Joliet. The Peoria end stops about 3 km short of the city centre, where the modern Iowa Interstate subdivision ends on the east bank; the Rock Island's own Peoria station approach is not drawn."
   },
